G.append({'heading': 'Front page', 'blocks': [
 p(' 1. The reopen inherits a hot print. The exchanges reopen at 9:15 AM after Diwali Balipratipada, and the session has one job: digest an American inflation report that landed while Mumbai was shut. What the holiday preserved is Monday\'s board - the Sensex at 81,106.65 and the Nifty at 25,044.65, a nine-month closing high, with the PSU Bank index up 3.7% and foreigners buying Rs 7,212 crore on the provisional print (' + a(CNBCFII, 'CNBC-TV18') + '). What arrived during the break: October CPI at plus 0.4% month-on-month on the headline, hotter than the 0.3% expected (story 2), a ten-year Treasury yield at 4.98% that grazed 5% overnight, and GIFT Nifty signalling only a flat start as of about 6 AM - an implied open near 25,057 (' + a(GIFT, 'giftniftytoday.com') + ', an aggregator\'s indication, not a price). The street\'s own map, per moneycontrol\'s trade setup: immediate support at 24,800, resistance at 25,200 and then 25,500 (' + a(MCSETUP, 'moneycontrol') + ' - analyst levels, attributed). Editor\'s takeaway: a flat indicated open into a hot print is itself information - the first hour decides whether Monday\'s PSU bank jump was a springboard or a trapdoor, and tonight\'s flow print scores it.'),
 p(' 2. The print itself: hotter on both lines, annual rates absent. The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported October CPI at plus 0.4% month-on-month on the headline - against plus 0.3% expected and plus 0.6% in September - with core, the line the Federal Reserve watches, at plus 0.3% against plus 0.2% expected (' + a(CNBCCPI, 'CNBC') + '). The report carried no year-on-year rates: the BLS did not collect October 2025 prices during the government shutdown, so the annual comparison does not exist this month. The market read it as a December story: the hotter core "increased expectations the Federal Reserve will raise rates again next month" (' + a(REUTNVDA, 'Reuters') + '). That is the call Wells Fargo\'s economists had riding on the core line, carried here on Tuesday. Yields did the talking: the ten-year rose twelve basis points to 4.98%, and the dollar index firmed to 102.26 (' + a(FNARENA, 'FNArena\'s overnight table') + '). India\'s own October inflation lands tomorrow: an FE poll of economists sees 6.1%, a twenty-four-month high (carried from yesterday). Editor\'s takeaway: with the annual rates missing, every participant is trading the same two month-on-month numbers - which is why a 0.1-percentage-point miss on core moved a 5% yield.'),
 p(' 3. New York took the print on the chin - orderly, not panicked. Tuesday\'s finals: the S&P 500 lost 29.01 points, or 0.38%, to 7,520.93; the Dow fell 157.86, or 0.30%, to 52,231.89; the Nasdaq dropped 187.26, or 0.74%, to 25,065.88 (' + a(FNARENA, 'FNArena') + '; ' + a(APWRAP, 'AP\'s wrap') + ' agrees on the percentages). The character of the day matters more than the size: the VIX actually fell, to 19.49, and the Dow and S&P never approached last week\'s damage. The fall\'s centre of gravity was one stock: Nvidia was down six and a half percent in afternoon trade (' + a(REUTNVDA, 'Reuters') + ') and closed at a near-yearly low (' + a(BBCNVDA, 'BBC') + ') after the company hired outside counsel to investigate its CEO (story 6). Around it, the AI complex wobbled with it - Dell down 4.8% on the day though still up 161% for the year, Sandisk down 4.5% and up 257% for the year, per LSEG data in the Reuters wrap. Editor\'s takeaway: a 0.4% index fall with a falling fear gauge is a repricing, not a rout - but the repricing is concentrated exactly where India\'s IT complex and the AI trade overlap, which is what the reopen inherits.'),
 p(' 4. Oil\'s second day at a two-month low - and the physical market agrees. Brent settled down $1.08, or 1.2%, at $91.49 a barrel on Tuesday; WTI settled down 81 cents, or 0.9%, at $86.04 - both benchmarks at their lowest since 31 August for a second straight session and technically oversold (' + a(EUROIL, 'Reuters\' settle report') + '). The intraday low was deeper: Brent briefly traded under $90 before recovering (' + a(CNBCOIL, 'CNBC') + '). The recovery\'s trigger was a denial, not a deal: Mohsen Rezaei, a senior Iranian security figure, told Al-Mayadeen that no negotiations with Washington are currently taking place. In Paris, where the G7 met, Secretary of State Rubio said the sanctions and the naval blockade are working, and that the president therefore does not need major combat operations. Gold felt the same unwind: front-month Comex settled down $84.30, or 2.05%, at $4,020.70 - the largest one-day fall, in dollars and percentage, since 30 October (' + a(MSGOLD, 'Dow Jones Data Talk, via Morningstar') + '). Editor\'s takeaway: two settles under $92 with dated cargoes below futures is no longer a war-premium leak, it is a market pricing peace it has not seen signed - the thing that reprices it is a text, a strait schedule, or a strike.'),
 p(' 5. The other table: four hours in Abu Dhabi. The first of two days of trilateral talks between Ukraine, Russia and the United States concluded in Abu Dhabi on Tuesday after four hours (' + a(BBCAD, 'BBC') + '). The principals stayed quiet; the proxies spoke. President Zelensky, who was not in the room, called the talks "constructive and quite substantive"; Secretary Rubio, from Paris, called them "very productive". US officials said Moscow and Kyiv are privately showing "a little bit of flexibility" on the postwar control of Ukraine\'s eastern regions - the question the whole negotiation turns on - while expectations of a breakthrough remain low. Day two is today. India has a seat at the edges of this table: Prime Minister Modi reaffirmed support for a peaceful resolution as the talks opened, and External Affairs Minister Jaishankar discussed the conflict with Russia\'s Foreign Minister Lavrov on Tuesday (' + a(AIR, 'All India Radio\'s bulletin') + '). Editor\'s takeaway: between the Gulf freeze and the Abu Dhabi table, Washington is running two endgames at once - and oil, gold and the rupee are all being asked to price both.'),
 p(' 6. The committee gets its counsel: Wachtell. Nvidia said on Tuesday it has hired Wachtell, Lipton, Rosen & Katz - the New York firm that writes the playbook for boards in crisis - with attorney Joon Kim leading what the company called "a thorough and independent investigation" into CEO Jensen Huang\'s relationship with an alleged Chinese intelligence asset; the FBI is running its own probe in parallel (' + a(BBCNVDA, 'BBC') + '). The day\'s additions to the file: the New York Times reports that Secretary Rubio sent a memo to senior officials earlier this year warning that Huang was in a close relationship with a Chinese spy; President Trump said Monday he had spoken to Huang, who, by Trump\'s account, told him he had no idea she was a spy - Trump said he believed him; Beijing\'s foreign ministry called the allegations "intended to smear and malign China". Nvidia\'s own lines are unchanged: the Megaspeed diversion concern was investigated and debunked, and the US government never informed the company of an intelligence concern. The market\'s verdict was story 3\'s: down six and a half percent in afternoon trade, a near-yearly low at the close. The earnings print is 17 November. Editor\'s takeaway: hiring Wachtell is the board admitting this is a governance question, not a news-cycle question - the mandate\'s exact wording, when it surfaces, is still the first tell to watch.'),
]})

