G.append({'heading': 'Finance Goldmine', 'blocks': [
 p('Fifteen sessions of foreign buying in a row - the Muhurat hour\'s token sale aside - and Friday\'s print is now confirmed at scale. Monday\'s provisional foreign-flow number - plus Rs 7,212 crore, reported on the night (' + a(CNBCFII, 'CNBC-TV18') + ') - extended a run that Friday\'s provisional Rs 13,749.82 crore had taken past Rs 48,000 crore in five sessions (CNBC-TV18). Monday evening also landed the custodians\' confirmed tally: NSDL\'s daily FPI table records gross purchases of Rs 32,089.60 crore against gross sales of Rs 17,360.23 crore, a net of Rs 14,729.37 crore on the stock exchanges, plus Rs 38.36 crore in the primary market (' + a(NSDL, 'NSDL\'s own table') + ') - but read the note under the table: those are trades conducted on 6 and 8 November, Friday and the Muhurat hour, reported a session later. So Friday\'s provisional 13,749.82 is now confirmed by the custodians, with the holiday hour inside the same number; Monday\'s own confirmation lands later this week. The two machines measure different days: the provisional print is compiled by the exchanges from trading members during the session; the custodial figure is compiled from depository reports and is the confirmed record - NSDL says exactly that on the page. What the confirmed tally upgrades is the story this paper has tracked for three weeks: the foreign buying behind the nine-month high is not a trickle but a tide, and the domestic selling against it - Rs 7,173 crore on Monday\'s provisional print - is being absorbed with room to spare. The rupee printed its version of the same tension on Monday: a six-month high of 93.69 intraday, then a close at 93.9275 as state-run banks bought dollars (' + a(ETRUPEE, 'Reuters, via ET CFO') + '). Foreigners are buying the equity; someone official is buying the dollars.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Startup Intelligence', 'blocks': [
 p('The 45-point argument, now with the expense line attached. Honasa Consumer\'s September quarter is becoming the consumer-internet sector\'s cleanest case study: the Mamaearth parent\'s advertising spend rose 36.1% year-on-year to Rs 245 crore - 33.4% of a revenue line that itself grew 35.9% to Rs 731 crore - while the retail footprint reached 3.2 lakh outlets and the focus categories grew over 40% to more than 80% of the business (' + a(SB18, 'Storyboard18\'s read of the quarter') + '). That is the detail underneath the analyst split carried here on Tuesday - Jefferies seeing 45% upside, Citi saying Sell: both houses are reading the same rupee, one as brand-building that compounds, one as growth bought at thirty-three paise on the rupee. The omnichannel push is the company\'s answer to the question - a digital-first brand spending its way into general trade. Elsewhere in the sector: Froncort.AI raised $2.3 million from Google.org to expand its AI-based disease-surveillance work (' + a(INC42, 'Inc42') + ') - small money, but the philanthropy arm of a frontier lab funding Indian health-AI is a signal about where the grant pipelines now point. What to watch in the prints ahead is unchanged: not the profit line, which both bulls and bears can explain, but the expense line underneath it.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'AI Race', 'blocks': [
 p('The week the machines started checking the proofs. Google DeepMind announced AlphaProof Code on Tuesday: an agentic framework that formally verified the actual deployed code of the Signal protocol - the cryptography under billions of private conversations - which the lab calls a grand challenge of software security (' + a(DEEPMIND, 'DeepMind\'s own post') + ', Pushmeet Kohli and Swarat Chaudhuri). Two firsts are claimed: the first time AI has formally verified a widely deployed cryptographic protocol at this depth, and the first time the implementation of such a protocol - not just its design - has been verified by any method, human or machine. The mechanics: human experts fixed what to prove; agents powered by a model the post calls Gemini Carbon generated more than six million lines of Lean, the proof language; the agents then audited their own work and flagged just one percent of the codebase for human second look. The whole verification took four weeks against the years expert teams have historically needed - and as a control, the researchers reintroduced two real, previously fixed bugs into an isolated copy; in both cases the system produced a machine-checked proof that the buggy version was insecure. It pairs with Monday\'s Anthropic note: Fable 5.2 and Mythos 5.2 formalised the proof of Fermat\'s Last Theorem in thirty hours in prerelease testing (' + a(ANTHRO, 'Anthropic') + ') - the commercial wrap on those models is $8 per million input tokens and $40 per million output, thirty percent faster than the 5.1 generation. Editor\'s takeaway: the two labs just spent the same week on the same idea - AI that produces proofs a machine can check, so the human audit shrinks to one percent of the work; whoever trusts that one percent is the new bottleneck of the whole field.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Vibe coding', 'blocks': [
 p('Your agent writes 2019 JavaScript because 2019 is what it read. A Chrome-team project that hit the front page of Hacker News overnight - 239 points at this writing (' + a(HNITEM, 'Show HN, by Chrome\'s Paul Irish') + ') - names a problem every builder with a coding agent has seen without naming: browsers now do natively much of what used to take fifty lines of JavaScript or a dependency - text-range highlighting, modal dialogs with proper focus handling, textareas that grow as you type - and the models know these features exist but reliably default to the older JavaScript-heavy pattern, because that is what their training years rewarded. The fix is not a better model; it is better context: the team is shipping a 1.0 of curated implementation guides - over two hundred of them, about 1.7 megabytes of hand-written best practice - built with Firefox, MDN and Edge, that supplement the agent with the platform knowledge its training never taught it to prefer. The lesson generalises past the browser. Every domain has a 2019 - the way the internet used to do it, over-represented in everything the model read. The builder\'s job is to be the correction layer: know the modern primitive exists, insist on it, and audit the diff. The agents are not behind because they are stupid; they are behind because the corpus is. The human who keeps the corpus honest keeps the edge.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Try this today (Wednesday)', 'blocks': [
 p('Watch the reopen like a desk, not a ticker. At 9:15 AM, one number settles three days of argument: the Nifty\'s first hour against Monday\'s 25,044.65 close, with 24,800 as the support the street published this morning. Then do the quiet half of the work: read one primary document - today\'s is short and free. Open ' + a(NSDL, 'NSDL\'s daily FPI table') + ' and find the row dated 9 November: gross purchases of Rs 32,089.60 crore, gross sales of Rs 17,360.23 crore, net plus Rs 14,729.37 crore. Then read the note under the table: those are Friday\'s and the Muhurat hour\'s trades, confirmed a session later - which is why it sits near Friday\'s provisional print of plus Rs 13,749.82 crore, not Monday\'s plus Rs 7,212 crore. That gap - what the exchange says in the evening against what the custodians confirm later - is the difference between the news and the record, and once you have seen it you will read every flow headline differently. If the Infosys Rs 26 dividend is yours, it pays today. And by evening, day two in Abu Dhabi will have ended - the wires will say whether "a little bit of flexibility" became text.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'One concept: the confirmed tally', 'blocks': [
 p('Every Indian trading evening produces a foreign-flow number, and almost every one is provisional. Here is the machine behind it. During the session, brokers report their trades to the exchanges; by evening the exchanges have compiled what trading members say foreign investors bought and sold - that is the provisional FII print, the one on television by 7 PM. Separately, the custodians - the banks that actually hold foreign investors\' shares and settle their trades - report to the depositories, and NSDL publishes the custodial tally: gross purchases, gross sales, net investment, in rupees and dollars. That second number is the confirmed record; NSDL states the distinction on its own page: exchange data comes from trading members on T-day, custodial data from depository reports. The two usually rhyme; last week they gave a clean demonstration. Friday\'s provisional print was plus Rs 13,749.82 crore; on Monday evening the custodians\' confirmed tally for Friday\'s and the Muhurat hour\'s trades landed at plus Rs 14,729.37 crore - the same story, one session later, with the holiday hour inside. Monday\'s own provisional print, plus Rs 7,212 crore, gets its custodial confirmation later this week. Block deals routed late, settlement corrections, and reporting lags all move trades from one bucket to the other; the direction of the gap tells you which side was still paperwork when the evening print froze. The reader\'s rule: the evening number is the story, the custodial number is the history. When they disagree, believe the custodians - and when the confirmed number lands bigger, as it did for Friday, the evening news had understated the day.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Term of the day: formal verification', 'blocks': [
 p('Testing shows a program works on the cases you tried; formal verification proves it works on all of them. The idea is old - mathematicians have wanted machine-checked certainty about software for decades - but the practice was brutal: an expert had to translate a program\'s intended behaviour into formal logic, then construct a proof, line by line, in a language like Lean, which checks every step mechanically. A large system could take a team years. That is what makes Tuesday\'s Signal result a milestone rather than a demo: DeepMind\'s agents generated the six million lines of Lean themselves, after humans fixed the specifications - the statements of what "secure" means - and a separate agent pass audited the proof and sent one percent of it back for human eyes (' + a(DEEPMIND, 'the lab\'s account') + '). Three words carry the whole term: specification (what to prove, still human), proof (the machine\'s part, checked by Lean, which trusts no one), and audit (the human\'s shrinking slice). The reason it matters beyond cryptography: every system that handles money, medicine or machines runs on code someone meant to write correctly. A tool that proves correctness - and, as DeepMind showed with the two reintroduced bugs, can also prove a known-bad version insecure - changes the economics of trusting software. The proof is the product; the code is just what it talks about.'),
]})

