lr_groups = [
 {'heading': '1. The ruin', 'blocks': [
  p('The story of India\'s largest tyre company begins with the destruction of a bank. Kandathil Mammen Mammen Mappillai was born in 1922 into the Kandathil family of Kerala - owners of the Malayala Manorama newspaper, a family that would collect five Padma awards across its generations. His father, K.C. Mammen Mappillai, ran the family\'s flagship, the Travancore National and Quilon Bank, and ran it into a feud with Sir C.P. Ramaswami Iyer, the autocratic Dewan of the princely state of Travancore. In 1938 the state liquidated the bank, and the father went to jail. The son, sixteen that year, finished his education at Madras Christian College sleeping on the bare floorboards of St. Thomas Hall (' + a(MINT, 'Mint\'s profile') + ' of the founder carries the details).'),
  p('Every empire has its founding humiliation; this one had a family honour to reclaim. When Mammen Mappillai set up his tiny unit in a rented shed in Tiruvottiyur, on the coast of north Madras, in 1946, he was, in Mint\'s phrase, building less a company than a restoration. The capital was Rs 10,000. The product was a toy balloon that sold for twenty-three paise.'),
 ]},
 {'heading': '2. The shed', 'blocks': [
  p('Tiruvottiyur was then part of Madras\'s auto belt, a neighbourhood of small workshops and fishermen. In the shed, Mappillai and his wife - Meera Kunjamma, a chemist by training, later described as MRF\'s first employee - made toy balloons by hand, and he took them out to sell (' + a(FORBES, 'Forbes India') + '). By 1949 the shed was turning out latex-cast squeaking toys, industrial gloves and contraceptives, and the business took its first office at 334, Thambu Chetty Street in the city\'s old commercial quarter (' + a(MRFTYRES, 'the company\'s own milestones') + '). One note on dates: the company, the Economic Times\' corporate history and every older account date the founding to 1946; a 2023 ' + a(NDTVP, 'NDTV Profit profile') + ' dates it to 1949, which is when the product range widened. This paper carries the company\'s own year.'),
  p('The name was the address of the ambition: Madras Rubber Factory. There were no machines yet - the "factory" of the late forties was a shed with workers\' hands. But the science graduate and the chemist were learning rubber: how latex cures, how a compound behaves, what makes one batch squeak and another crack. When the pivot came, they were ready for it.'),
 ]},
 {'heading': '3. The pivot', 'blocks': [
  p('In 1952 Mappillai noticed something in his own family: a cousin ran tyre-retreading plants, and the tread rubber those plants consumed - the strip of fresh rubber vulcanised onto a worn tyre to give it a second life - was imported (' + a(MINT, 'Mint') + '). He installed the shed\'s first machine, a rubber mill, and began making tread rubber. The multinationals who owned India\'s rubber market - Dunlop, Goodyear, Firestone - treated the Indian entrant as a curiosity.'),
  p('Four years later they were gone from the segment. By 1955 Madras Rubber Factory was the only Indian-owned unit making the superior extruded, non-blooming, cushion-backed tread rubber (' + a(ETHIST, 'ET\'s corporate history') + '); by the close of 1956 it held fifty percent of India\'s tread-rubber market, and the multinationals had withdrawn from the business rather than fight for it (' + a(MRFTYRES, 'the company\'s milestones') + '). It is the pattern the whole later story repeats: enter underneath the incumbents, win on compound quality, and hold the segment until the economics of fighting stop making sense.'),
 ]},
 {'heading': '4. Tyres', 'blocks': [
  p('The jump from tread rubber to tyres is the jump from repairing a product to making it - plant, chemistry, capital and brand, all at once. Mappillai made it with a partner: on 5 November 1960 the company was incorporated as a private limited company, manufacturing automobile, aircraft and cycle tyres in technical collaboration with the Mansfield Tire & Rubber Company of Mansfield, Ohio - the early tyres were sold under the trade name "Mansfield Tyres (MRF)" (' + a(ETHIST, 'ET\'s history') + '). In 1961 MRF went public, and Tamil Nadu\'s Chief Minister, K. Kamaraj, released the first tyre from the new pilot plant at Tiruvottiyur. The state kept blessing the factory: Industries Minister R. Venkataraman commissioned a new unit in December 1962 (' + a(HINDU, 'The Hindu\'s archive') + '), and in June 1963 Prime Minister Nehru laid the foundation stone of its rubber research centre.'),
  p('Then the company did something no Indian tyre maker had done: it sold to the Americans. In 1964 MRF opened an overseas office in Beirut - one of India\'s first serious export sorties - and in 1967 it became the first Indian company to export tyres to the United States, the birthplace of tyre technology (' + a(NDTVP, 'NDTV Profit') + '). The Beirut year also gave India an enduring piece of commercial art: the MRF Muscleman, the bare-chested figure holding a tyre aloft, born 1964, which turned an industrial product into a household idea of strength (' + a(MINT, 'Mint') + '). By 1970 the company had its second plant. The balloon shed was now a multinational\'s problem.'),
 ]},
 {'heading': '5. The sieges', 'blocks': [
  p('Inside the gates, Mappillai ran a patriarchal, prayerful enterprise - major decisions preceded by prayer, absolute loyalty demanded, secrecy absolute, quality uncompromised, with his brothers K.M. Cherian, K.M. Philip and K.M. Mathew as the close circle (' + a(MINT, 'Mint\'s account') + '). Outside the gates, the quality had made MRF worth owning - and twice, someone tried to own it.'),
  p('The first siege came from a friend. Michelin, the French giant, was MRF\'s partner in aircraft tyres - the company had climbed from balloons to fighter-aircraft tyres in four decades - and in the early 1990s it moved to take control of its partner. Mappillai, whose distrust of multinationals was by then doctrine, fought the grab and won by rallying India\'s domestic financial institutions behind the promoter\'s stake. A second hostile attempt came in 2000, and he saw that one off too (' + a(MINT, 'Mint') + '). The control never left the family; it has not since.'),
  p('The same stubbornness wrote the most famous number in the Indian market. MRF\'s promoters consistently refused to split the stock, preferring operating performance to market optics (' + a(MINT, 'Mint') + '), and the float stayed tiny - a little over three million shares against, for comparison, nearly two billion at TCS (' + a(SCROLL, 'Scroll.in') + '). So the price compounded in plain sight: Rs 1,218 in January 2001; the first Indian share ever to cross Rs 60,000, in March 2017; the first to cross one lakh, in June 2023; and, at Monday\'s BSE close, Rs 1,25,070.85 for a single share with a face value of ten rupees. The price is not the strategy; it is the receipt.'),
 ]},
 {'heading': '6. The ledger', 'blocks': [
  p('K.M. Mammen Mappillai died in 2003. The arithmetic of his life\'s work: from a Rs 10,000 balloon venture to revenues of Rs 2,241 crore in his last year - and, in the financial year just ended, Rs 31,654 crore, with roughly thirty percent of India\'s tyre market, ahead of Apollo and CEAT at home and Bridgestone and Michelin from abroad (' + a(WIKI, 'the company\'s current figures') + '; ' + a(MINT, 'Mint') + '). Brand Finance ranks MRF the world\'s second-strongest tyre brand. The family still runs it: sons K.M. Mammen and Arun Mammen, and a fourth generation led by managing director Rahul Mammen Mappillai.'),
  p('The second identity is cricket\'s. In 1987 the company founded the MRF Pace Foundation in Chennai - associated with Dennis Lillee and later with a young Glenn McGrath - giving a tyre company the kind of cultural footing the Michelin Guide gives a restaurant guide\'s parent (' + a(MINT, 'Mint\'s comparison') + '). Generations of Indian fast bowlers came through its gates while the Muscleman held his tyre over the boundary boards.'),
  p('And on Tuesday, on a day the market was shut, the company this paper can finally tell in full filed its quarter: revenue up 17.4% to Rs 8,660 crore, consolidated net profit down 26.6% to Rs 387 crore on the company\'s own headline line - the other line of the same package, total comprehensive income attributable to owners, fell 22.8% to Rs 417 crore - as rubber costs jumped 43% year-on-year; and an interim dividend of three rupees a share, declared into the headwind (' + a(BSEMRF, 'the filing itself') + '; ' + a(ETMRF, 'ETMarkets') + '; ' + a(ACPMRF, 'Autocar Professional') + '). A down quarter absorbed with the dividend held is not a new scene in this story; it is the same scene, the eighty-first year of it. Somewhere in the file of 1938 there is a liquidated bank and a boy on floorboards; the institution that boy built now weathers commodity cycles the way he taught it to - quietly, with the books closed to optics and the doors open to no one.'),
 ]},
]

